Abstract = {Currently, cardiovascular disease represents the main cause of morbidity
   and mortality worldwide; the application of nanotechnology holds great
   promise for its prevention and treatment. Nanodevices ({''}smart
   drugs{''}) are currently being developed for directed and controlled
   delivery of drugs to specific sites in the body, such as cells, tissues,
   blood vessels and the heart, as well as for diagnosis and early
   detection of cardiovascular conditions and patient-individualized
   treatment. Another application of nanodevices is the delivery of drugs
   to correct defective protein bad coupling or binding. With potent
   super-effects, nanoparticles should be able to elicit therapeutic
   effects at lower doses and prolonged periods. The manufacture of
   nanodevices and nanocarriers should be with a comprehensive approach
   that takes general properties into account in order to assess for
   biocompatibility and, therefore, avoid adverse and toxic effects.
   Intensified research in this field will help to significantly reduce
   morbidity and mortality caused by cardiovascular disease.},
